WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,313
Hardening begins with knowing what systems exist.

2
00:00:03,313 --> 00:00:17,874
A useful inventory includes workstations, laptops, servers, virtual machines, cloud instances, containers, appliances, administrative systems, lab systems, and endpoint devices that may not be centrally managed yet.

3
00:00:17,874 --> 00:00:29,199
It should record the system owner, business purpose, operating system, role, location, exposure, installed software where practical, and whether the system is supported.

4
00:00:29,199 --> 00:00:32,203
Unknown systems cannot be hardened reliably.

5
00:00:32,203 --> 00:00:35,439
Ownership turns discovery into accountability.

6
00:00:35,439 --> 00:00:44,145
Every system should have a team or person responsible for decisions about configuration, patching, access, backup, and retirement.

7
00:00:44,145 --> 00:00:46,995
Without an owner, remediation work stalls.

8
00:00:46,995 --> 00:00:54,622
A server may need a patch, an endpoint protection agent may be unhealthy, or a remote access exception may be too broad,

9
00:00:54,622 --> 00:00:57,319
but no one can approve or schedule the fix.

10
00:00:57,319 --> 00:01:02,249
Hardening requires someone who understands the system's purpose and risk.

11
00:01:02,249 --> 00:01:05,023
Classification helps prioritize effort.

12
00:01:05,023 --> 00:01:21,047
Production systems, development systems, internet-facing systems, internal systems, systems with sensitive data, administrative workstations, ordinary user endpoints, and low-risk lab devices do not all need the same urgency or controls.

13
00:01:21,047 --> 00:01:31,833
Classification helps the team decide where stricter baselines, faster patching, stronger monitoring, encryption, or tighter access controls are most important.

14
00:01:31,833 --> 00:01:33,836
Inventory is a living control.

15
00:01:33,836 --> 00:01:43,775
New cloud instances appear, laptops are reassigned, containers are rebuilt, software is installed, and old systems remain online longer than planned.

16
00:01:43,775 --> 00:01:52,865
Regular inventory review helps teams find shadow systems, unsupported software, missing endpoint protection, and systems with no owner.

17
00:01:52,865 --> 00:02:00,647
It also gives hardening work a safer starting point because teams can make decisions from evidence rather than guesses.
